{
  "dom_id": "step-q111209",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257398/assets/78b79bedf111a77f762bd0eda04d11579f2052985fcf8146d791d6377475e273.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(3x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
  "worked_solution": "To graph the function $y=f(3x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257398/assets/079d53cdedcb13625f94b32fa1bb7a3a78a0986d600a1795aefd3ef5cc163016.png)",
